; the chemistry of glass formation and types.
; 
; FORMATION: silica network + network modifiers
; pure SiO2: melts at 1700°C. too high for industrial production.
; Na2O added (soda): breaks Si-O-Si network → lowers melting point to 850°C.
; CaO added (lime): restores chemical durability (soda glass is water-soluble).
; soda-lime glass: 72% SiO2, 14% Na2O, 9% CaO — window and bottle glass.
; 
; NETWORK STRUCTURE:
; SiO4 tetrahedra linked at corners — 3D covalent network (like quartz).
; Na⁺, Ca²⁺ ions sit in the network gaps as network modifiers.
; no long-range order (amorphous) — no crystal lattice.
; this is why glass has no sharp melting point: softens gradually (Tg ≈ 550°C).
; glass is a supercooled liquid: the network is frozen in disordered state.
; 
; TYPES:
; soda-lime:     windows, bottles, jars. cheap. Tg ≈ 550°C.
; borosilicate:  B2O3 added. lower thermal expansion. Pyrex. lab glassware.
; resists thermal shock: can heat rapidly without cracking.
; lead crystal:  PbO added. high refractive index (1.56+). heavy. optical.
; tempered:      rapid quench from 650°C → surface compression → 4× stronger.
; breaks into small cubes (safe) not shards. car windows.
; laminated:     two glass layers + PVB polymer interlayer. breaks but holds together.
; windscreens. safety glass.
; low-e:         thin metallic coating reflects infrared → insulating glazing.
; 
; PROPERTIES:
; transparent: photon energies below bandgap → no absorption in visible range.
; hard (6-7 Mohs) but brittle: no plastic deformation, crack propagates.
; chemically inert: resists most acids (except HF which attacks Si-O bonds).
; excellent thermal insulator in bulk (k = 1.0 W/mK).

